</table>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table"><span id="A0.T2.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Table 2</span>: </span><span id="A0.T2.5" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Comparison of trajectory fitting methods on complex motion video. Ridge regression provides the best trade-off between accuracy and stability.</span></figcaption>
</figure>
<section id="A1" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix A </span>Feasibility Proof of Polynomial Approximation</h2>

<div id="A1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">This appendix provides the detailed mathematical proof supporting the feasibility of representing control point motion using polynomial functions.</p>
</div>
<div id="A1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A1.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 1.</span> If a Bézier curve changes continuously over time, then its control points must also vary continuously.</p>
</div>
<div id="A1.Thmtheorem1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="A1.Thmtheorem1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proof A.1</span></span><span id="A1.Thmtheorem1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="A1.Thmtheorem1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.Thmtheorem1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.Thmtheorem1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Assuming that the Bézier curve changes continuously over time, let the curve at time <math id="A1.Thmtheorem1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math> be <math id="A1.Thmtheorem1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C(u,t)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>u</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C(u,t)</annotation></semantics></math>. Since the Bernstein basis functions are linearly independent, the control points <math id="A1.Thmtheorem1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{P_{0},P_{1},...,P_{m}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>m</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{P_{0},P_{1},...,P_{m}\}</annotation></semantics></math> can be uniquely determined from the curve <math id="A1.Thmtheorem1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C(u)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C(u)</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="A1.Thmtheorem1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.Thmtheorem1.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.Thmtheorem1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">For a set of distinct values <math id="A1.Thmtheorem1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>u</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u</annotation></semantics></math>, e.g. <math id="A1.Thmtheorem1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u_{0},u_{1},...,u_{m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>u</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>m</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u_{0},u_{1},...,u_{m}</annotation></semantics></math>, if the curve <math id="A1.Thmtheorem1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C(u)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C(u)</annotation></semantics></math> is continuous with respect to <math id="A1.Thmtheorem1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math> for each <math id="A1.Thmtheorem1.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>u</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u</annotation></semantics></math>, then the corresponding points on the curve <math id="A1.Thmtheorem1.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C(u_{0}),C(u_{1}),...,C(u_{m})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C(u_{0}),C(u_{1}),...,C(u_{m})</annotation></semantics></math> are also continuous with respect to <math id="A1.Thmtheorem1.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math>. These points on the curve are linear combinations of the control points, which we can express as:</span></p>
<table id="A1.Ex1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A1.Ex1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\begin{bmatrix}C(u_{0},t)\\
C(u_{1},t)\\
\vdots\\
C(u_{m},t)\end{bmatrix}=\begin{bmatrix}B_{m,0}(u_{0})&amp;B_{m,1}(u_{0})&amp;\cdots&amp;B_{m,m}(u_{0})\\
B_{m,0}(u_{1})&amp;B_{m,1}(u_{1})&amp;\cdots&amp;B_{m,m}(u_{1})\\
\vdots&amp;\vdots&amp;\ddots&amp;\vdots\\
B_{m,0}(u_{m})&amp;B_{m,1}(u_{m})&amp;\cdots&amp;B_{m,m}(u_{m})\end{bmatrix}\cdot\begin{bmatrix}P_{0}(t)\\
P_{1}(t)\\
\vdots\\
P_{m}(t)\end{bmatrix}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo>[</mo><mtable displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo>]</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>[</mo><mtable columnspacing="5pt" displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd><mrow><msub><mi>B</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mtd><mtd><mrow><msub><mi>B</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mtd><mtd><mo>⋯</mo></mtd><mtd><mrow><msub><mi>B</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mrow><msub><mi>B</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mtd><mtd><mrow><msub><mi>B</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mtd><mtd><mo>⋯</mo></mtd><mtd><mrow><msub><mi>B</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd></mtd><mtd></mtd><mtd><mi mathvariant="normal">⋱</mi></mtd><mtd></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mrow><msub><mi>B</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mtd><mtd><mrow><msub><mi>B</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mtd><mtd><mo>⋯</mo></mtd><mtd><mrow><msub><mi>B</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo rspace="0.055em">]</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mtable displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\begin{bmatrix}C(u_{0},t)\\
C(u_{1},t)\\
\vdots\\
C(u_{m},t)\end{bmatrix}=\begin{bmatrix}B_{m,0}(u_{0})&amp;B_{m,1}(u_{0})&amp;\cdots&amp;B_{m,m}(u_{0})\\
B_{m,0}(u_{1})&amp;B_{m,1}(u_{1})&amp;\cdots&amp;B_{m,m}(u_{1})\\
\vdots&amp;\vdots&amp;\ddots&amp;\vdots\\
B_{m,0}(u_{m})&amp;B_{m,1}(u_{m})&amp;\cdots&amp;B_{m,m}(u_{m})\end{bmatrix}\cdot\begin{bmatrix}P_{0}(t)\\
P_{1}(t)\\
\vdots\\
P_{m}(t)\end{bmatrix}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="A1.Thmtheorem1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.Thmtheorem1.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.Thmtheorem1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Abbreviated as:</span></p>
<table id="A1.Ex2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A1.Ex2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{C}(t)=\mathbf{M}\cdot\mathbf{P}(t)" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>𝐂</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>𝐌</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mrow><mi>𝐏</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{C}(t)=\mathbf{M}\cdot\mathbf{P}(t)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="A1.Thmtheorem1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.Thmtheorem1.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.Thmtheorem1.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Here, <math id="A1.Thmtheorem1.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐌</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{M}</annotation></semantics></math> is an invertible matrix (when the <math id="A1.Thmtheorem1.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u_{k}</annotation></semantics></math> are chosen appropriately), allowing us to solve for the control points:</span></p>
<table id="A1.Ex3" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A1.Ex3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{P}(t)=\mathbf{M}^{-1}\cdot\mathbf{C}(t)" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>𝐏</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>𝐌</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mrow><mi>𝐂</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{P}(t)=\mathbf{M}^{-1}\cdot\mathbf{C}(t)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="A1.Thmtheorem1.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.Thmtheorem1.p5.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.Thmtheorem1.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Therefore, if the Bézier curve is continuous in <math id="A1.Thmtheorem1.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math>, its control points must also be continuous in <math id="A1.Thmtheorem1.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="A1.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A1.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 2.</span> A continuously changing Bézier curve can be arbitrarily approximated by a Bézier curve whose control points are polynomial functions of time <math id="A1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="A1.Thmtheorem2" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="A1.Thmtheorem2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proof A.2</span></span><span id="A1.Thmtheorem2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="A1.Thmtheorem2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.Thmtheorem2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.Thmtheorem2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">For an arbitrary control point <math id="A1.Thmtheorem2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P</annotation></semantics></math>, its 2D coordinate vector at time parameter <math id="A1.Thmtheorem2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t\in[0,1]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t\in[0,1]</annotation></semantics></math> can be represented by a function <math id="A1.Thmtheorem2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P(t)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P(t)</annotation></semantics></math>, which is continuous in <math id="A1.Thmtheorem2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math>. According to the Weierstrass approximation theorem <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx57" title="" class="ltx_ref">Sto48</a>]</cite>, any continuous function can be arbitrarily approximated by polynomials. Thus, for each control point <math id="A1.Thmtheorem2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{i}(t)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{i}(t)</annotation></semantics></math>, there exists a polynomial function <math id="A1.Thmtheorem2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q_{i}(t)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Q</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q_{i}(t)</annotation></semantics></math> (with time <math id="A1.Thmtheorem2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math> as the variable) such that <math id="A1.Thmtheorem2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|P_{i}(t)-Q_{i}(t)|&lt;\epsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>Q</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>ϵ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|P_{i}(t)-Q_{i}(t)|&lt;\epsilon</annotation></semantics></math> holds for all <math id="A1.Thmtheorem2.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t\in[0,1]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t\in[0,1]</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="A1.Thmtheorem2.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ϵ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon</annotation></semantics></math> is an arbitrarily small positive number.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="A1.Thmtheorem2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.Thmtheorem2.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.Thmtheorem2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Define the approximate Bézier curve represented by <math id="A1.Thmtheorem2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q_{i}(t)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Q</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q_{i}(t)</annotation></semantics></math> as <math id="A1.Thmtheorem2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D(u,t)=\sum_{i=0}^{m}B_{m,i}(u)Q_{i}(t)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>u</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mi>m</mi></msubsup><mrow><msub><mi>B</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>Q</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D(u,t)=\sum_{i=0}^{m}B_{m,i}(u)Q_{i}(t)</annotation></semantics></math>. Since the Bernstein basis satisfies <math id="A1.Thmtheorem2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sum_{i=0}^{m}B_{m,i}(u)=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mi>m</mi></msubsup><mrow><msub><mi>B</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sum_{i=0}^{m}B_{m,i}(u)=1</annotation></semantics></math> and is non-negative, for any <math id="A1.Thmtheorem2.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u,t\in[0,1]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u,t\in[0,1]</annotation></semantics></math>, we have:</span></p>
</div>
<div id="A1.Thmtheorem2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<table id="A1.EGx1" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="A1.Ex4"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A1.Ex4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle|C(u,t)-D(u,t)|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>u</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>u</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle|C(u,t)-D(u,t)|</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A1.Ex4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\left|\sum_{i=0}^{m}(P_{i}(t)-Q_{i}(t))B_{m,i}(u)\right|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>|</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mi>m</mi></munderover></mstyle><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>Q</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>B</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>|</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\left|\sum_{i=0}^{m}(P_{i}(t)-Q_{i}(t))B_{m,i}(u)\right|</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="A1.Ex5"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A1.Ex5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\leq\sum_{i=0}^{m}|P_{i}(t)-Q_{i}(t)|B_{m,i}(u)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mi>m</mi></munderover></mstyle><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>Q</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>B</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\leq\sum_{i=0}^{m}|P_{i}(t)-Q_{i}(t)|B_{m,i}(u)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="A1.Ex6"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A1.Ex6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\leq\epsilon\sum_{i=0}^{m}B_{m,i}(u)=\epsilon." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mi>ϵ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mi>m</mi></munderover></mstyle><mrow><msub><mi>B</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>ϵ</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\leq\epsilon\sum_{i=0}^{m}B_{m,i}(u)=\epsilon.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="A1.Thmtheorem2.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.Thmtheorem2.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.Thmtheorem2.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">This means the error between the approximate curve <math id="A1.Thmtheorem2.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D(u,t)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>u</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D(u,t)</annotation></semantics></math> and the original curve <math id="A1.Thmtheorem2.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C(u,t)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>u</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C(u,t)</annotation></semantics></math> does not exceed <math id="A1.Thmtheorem2.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ϵ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon</annotation></semantics></math> for any <math id="A1.Thmtheorem2.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u,t\in[0,1]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u,t\in[0,1]</annotation></semantics></math>. Therefore, a continuously changing Bézier curve can be arbitrarily approximated by a Bézier curve whose control points are polynomial functions of time <math id="A1.Thmtheorem2.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A2" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix B </span>Stroke trajectory initialization</h2>

<div id="A2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We compare three fitting strategies for initializing stroke control point motion trajectories: uniform sampling, least squares, and ridge regression.</p>
</div>
<div id="A2.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A2.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">polynomial interpolation</span> selects <math id="A2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n+1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n+1</annotation></semantics></math> frames from the video, uses normalized time <math id="A2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> as nodes and solves linear equations to ensure the trajectory passes exactly through sample points in these frames. Though fitting is accurate at sampled frames, fitting error increases significantly at non-sampled frames when <math id="A2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n&gt;15" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>15</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n&gt;15</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="A2.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A2.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Least squares</span> minimizes the sum of squared errors for polynomial coefficient estimation. However, when polynomial degree is high (<math id="A2.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n&gt;40" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>40</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n&gt;40</annotation></semantics></math>), the Vandermonde matrix becomes ill-conditioned <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx4" title="" class="ltx_ref">Bel78</a>]</cite>, making solutions highly sensitive to noise and numerically unstable.</p>
</div>
<div id="A2.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A2.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Ridge regression</span> introduces L2 regularization <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx27" title="" class="ltx_ref">HK70</a>]</cite> to address ill-conditioning in high-degree polynomial fitting (<math id="A2.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n&gt;200" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>200</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n&gt;200</annotation></semantics></math>). Although Mean Absolute Error (MAE) is similar between least squares and ridge regression, the former produces coefficients with extremely large magnitudes, leading to oscillatory behavior and overfitting. Ridge regression constrains coefficient magnitudes, yielding stable and smooth trajectories.</p>
</div>
<div id="A2.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.p5.1" class="ltx_p">Table <a href="#A0.T2" title="Table 2 ‣ Vector sketch animation generation with differentialable motion trajectories" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a> compares the three methods on the complex dance video under varying frame counts and polynomial degrees. Frame sampling shows large errors and coefficient magnitudes. Least squares achieves lower MAE but exhibits unstable trajectories due to large coefficients. Ridge regression maintains acceptable MAE (maximum 1.433 pixels) while keeping coefficient magnitudes around <math id="A2.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="10^{4}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mn>10</mn><mn>4</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">10^{4}</annotation></semantics></math>, demonstrating significantly better stability.</p>
</div>
</section>
</article>
</div>
</div>
